From: Salt OpenWrt packaging
Subject: [PATCH] crypt: retry authentication when the TCP stream closes

A master restart can close an in-flight authentication request. Unlike a
request timeout, StreamClosedError escapes sign_in and leaves the shared
authentication future pending forever. Use the existing bounded/backoff
retry path so future authentication callers can make progress. Do not catch
CancelledError: shutting down the minion must still cancel the operation.

On first startup singleton initialization creates minion.pem. Recompute the
cache key afterwards: its mtime is part of the identity. Otherwise publish
and request channels obtain different AsyncAuth objects, and invalidating
the publisher on a master restart leaves the requester using stale AES creds.

--- a/salt/crypt.py
+++ b/salt/crypt.py
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@
 
 import tornado.concurrent
 import tornado.ioloop
+import tornado.iostream
 
 import salt.cache
 import salt.channel.client
@@ -1087,6 +1088,9 @@
             # references it-- this forces a reference while we return to the caller
             auth = object.__new__(cls)
             auth.__singleton_init__(opts, io_loop=io_loop)
+            # Initialization may create the keypair; cache its final identity.
+            # Both request and publish channels must share this authenticator.
+            key = cls.__key(opts)
             loop_instance_map[key] = auth
         else:
             log.debug("Re-using AsyncAuth for %s", key)
@@ -1245,6 +1249,11 @@
                 attempts += 1
                 try:
                     creds = await self.sign_in(channel=channel)
+                except tornado.iostream.StreamClosedError:
+                    log.warning(
+                        "TCP connection to master closed during authentication; retrying"
+                    )
+                    creds = "retry"
                 except SaltClientError as exc:
                     error = exc
                     break
